## LOAD (binding; the MPS heavy-slot convention, RELAYED from the B524 and B453 kits)
8 cores. Heavy = `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, `dotnet test --list-tests`, `npm ci`, `npm test`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, a mutant matrix, the full CI line. **Start a heavy run only while `uptime` load1 is below 8** at two reads 4 s apart (they are not independent: say so), recorded beside the result; at 8 or over poll every 20 s, log each wait, and do the light work meanwhile (C1, C2 reading, the attack-row design, the oracle, C8, C9). **Then take ONE slot atomically:** `mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-1' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-2' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-3'`, write `B533 <start time> <label>` into its `holder`; read every other `heavy-slot-*/holder` first. A holder that looks stale: report it, never remove it. All taken: wait 3 minutes and retry (several seats share the slots: expect queueing). At most ONE slot, one heavy job at a time (a mutant matrix is one job; a filtered `dotnet test` per mutant is a heavy run inside the held slot); at the run's end (pass, fail or abort) remove ONLY your own slot (`holder` + `rmdir`, same block). The shared `.tools/.git-lock` wraps GIT REF WRITES ONLY (`fetch`, `worktree add`); never hold it across a build or a test. Write your OWN wrapper in `.tools/b533/` (never copy or run another seat's script). Before your last line `ls '<project root>/.tools/' | /usr/bin/grep heavy-slot` shows no holder naming B533. Never kill a process by name; only your own ancestry. NO artificial load. Use test filters for loops and the whole line once. The SDK is project-local `<project root>/.tools/dotnet`, run with `-nodeReuse:false -p:UseSharedCompilation=false` and `DOTNET_CLI_USE_MSBUILD_SERVER=0`; node 24 is CI's: record `node --version`, `npm --version` and say which you used (the builder used v26.8.1; `web/package.json` engines accept >= 20.19.0).

